{#
  Managed by Ansible — do not edit manually.

  These are Jinja comments deliberately: OpenCode validates its config and
  REJECTS unrecognized top-level keys ("Configuration is invalid ...
  Unrecognized keys"), so JSON-style "_comment" fields break the whole file
  and the agent refuses to run. Keys inside permission.bash are worse — that
  map is free-form, so a comment key there is accepted as a glob pattern whose
  permission value is a sentence rather than allow/ask/deny. Keep all prose
  here, where it never reaches the rendered file.

  TWO FOOTGUNS, verified against opencode.ai/docs/permissions:

  1. bash defaults to ALLOW. If no pattern matches and no "*" catch-all is
     defined, the command runs. Omitting the catch-all below leaves an open
     shell on a box holding keys trusted by all 7 hosts.

  2. Rules are LAST-MATCH-WINS, not first-match. "*": "deny" must therefore be
     declared FIRST, with the specific allows after it. Reversed, everything is
     denied and the sweep silently stops working.

  "ask" is deliberately unused. Headless it is not a control: with no TTY there
  is nobody to prompt, and `--auto` (which investigate.sh never passes) turns
  every "ask" into "allow". Anything that must not happen is denied outright.
  "ask" is reserved for the Phase C operator, where a human is present by
  construction.

  3. Every tool not named here defaults to ALLOW (OpenCode's built-in
     "*": "allow"), and that includes `read`, `grep`, `glob` and `list`.
     Forced 2026-10-12 (PER-112): a headless run's `read` tool opened
     ~/.agent/last_anomaly.json, so it could open anything else under
     /home/choco, which OpenCode treats as the project. The top-level
     "*": "deny" below comes FIRST and hides every tool whose last matching
     rule is a bare deny. Only bash survives, because its own rules come
     after it.

  Paths: a command that takes a file path (cat, ls, ...) is resolved, and a
  path outside /home/choco is "external_directory", which a headless run
  auto-rejects. Forced the same day: `cat ~/.agent/../../../etc/hostname` was
  refused. That is why the sweep's credentials live in /etc/agent/, not in
  ~/.agent/.

  "ssh -*" is denied last: options before the host (-F, -i, -E) make ssh read
  a local file of the model's choosing. A coarse filter, see below.

  Glob matching on a command string is a coarse filter, not a security
  boundary. The real constraint on "ssh *-agent *" is the key itself: pinned to
  10.30.40.203, mapped to a read-only account, revocable fleet-wide in one run.
#}
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",

  "model": "{{ agent_monitor_model }}",
  "small_model": "{{ agent_small_model }}",

  "agent": {
    "infra-monitor": {
      "steps": {{ agent_max_steps }}
    }
  },

  "permission": {
    "*": "deny",
    "edit": "deny",
    "write": "deny",
    "webfetch": "deny",

    "bash": {
      "*": "deny",
      "ssh *-agent *": "allow",
      "cat {{ agent_state_dir }}/*": "allow",
      "ls {{ agent_state_dir }}*": "allow",
      "date*": "allow",
      "ssh -*": "deny"
    }
  },

  "provider": {
    "anthropic": {
      "options": {
        "timeout": {{ agent_provider_timeout_ms }}
      }
    }{% if vault_fireworks_api_key is defined %},
    "fireworks": {
      "options": {
        "timeout": {{ agent_provider_timeout_ms }}
      }
    }{% endif %}

  }
}
